<body><div class="doc">

<div class="center" style="padding:14px 0 4px;text-align:center">
  <div class="eyebrow">FemWell · Community · FEATURE #4 · CIRCLES · brainstorm (build paused for your reaction)</div>
  <h1 class="cover">Believed here</h1>
  <p class="lede" style="font-style:italic;max-width:560px;margin:8px auto 0">Circles are the smaller rooms — for <b>who you are</b> (life stages), <b>what you're living with</b> (PCOS, endo, PMDD), and <b>what you love</b> (books, career, creativity, movement). This is the deep brainstorm for making them robust, warm and safe. The best of them give a woman one thing a big room can't: "here it's <i>believed</i>." Cited research inside; <b>nothing built yet — this is the plan for your reaction.</b></p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <div class="eyebrow">The one-line thesis</div>
  <p class="lede">The killer for a small community isn't too few features — it's an <b>empty circle</b>. So Circles v1 is about <b>aliveness and warmth</b>: every live circle is <b>seeded so it's never at zero</b>, has a <b>Jess host</b> who guarantees a first reply, a <b>warm first-post welcome</b> ("you're not too much"), a <b>gentle whole-life discovery</b> card (a Creativity circle beside a Menopause one), and — for the sensitive ones — a calm <b>NHS-grounded info anchor</b> + an author-set <b>content-warning veil</b>. Almost all of it runs on entities we already have.</p>
</div>

<div class="section sage">
  <div class="eyebrow">Contents</div>
  <div class="toc">
    <a href="#now"><span>1</span> What Circles are today</a>
    <a href="#evidence"><span>2</span> The evidence (cited)</a>
    <a href="#v1"><span>3</span> The robust v1 — 7 pillars</a>
    <a href="#sensitive"><span>4</span> Sensitive circles — safe &amp; warm</a>
    <a href="#build"><span>5</span> Build reality + what needs sign-off</a>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- 1 -->
<a id="now"></a>
<div class="section crimson">
  <div class="eyebrow">1 · Starting point (already built)</div>
  <h2>What Circles are today</h2>
  <p>A curated catalogue of <b>12 circles</b> in three groups — <b>Life stages</b> (TTC · pregnancy · postpartum · perimenopause · menopause), <b>Living with</b> (PCOS · endo · PMDD, flagged sensitive with consent-on-join), <b>Shared loves</b> (books · career · creativity · movement). Each is lurkable (read without joining), join-to-post, with a device-local join and a \`CircleMembership\` entity; posts are circle-scoped \`CommunityPost\`; discovery already suggests circles from life-stage + interests. The bones are right — this feature makes them <b>alive and warm</b>, not just present.</p>
</div>

<!-- 2 -->
<a id="evidence"></a>
<div class="section">
  <div class="eyebrow">2 · The evidence (verified sources)</div>
  <h2>What makes a cohort work</h2>
  <div class="lens"><div class="nm"><span class="ix">Aliveness</span>Empty = death; variety of voices is the survival signal</div><p>A community needs only <b>10–20 active people to feel alive</b>, but only ~10–30% show to any thread — so too many circles dilutes below the floor. The earliest survival predictor is <b>poster variety, visible within 2 hours</b> of a channel's life; the killer is a no-recent-posts feed that reads "dead." <b>Seed every live circle.</b></p><div class="src">medium.com/together-institute · dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1718918.1718932 (ACM CSCW)</div></div>
  <div class="lens"><div class="nm"><span class="ix">"Believed here"</span>The specific benefit of a condition cohort</div><p>PCOS study: women valued talking "with people who completely understand," got information their clinicians lacked, and felt "want to fight it even more." That's the exact promise of the endo/PCOS/PMDD circles — validation you can't get elsewhere.</p><div class="src">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/PMC3867626</div></div>
  <div class="lens"><div class="nm"><span class="ix">Curated</span>Curated beats user-created for a small, safe app</div><p>Mumsnet is staff-curated topics; HealthUnlocked condition communities are charity-hosted + moderated. Curation removes creation-moderation, dead-circle sprawl and vanity surfaces. <b>Keep it curated</b> — don't add a "create a circle" button.</p><div class="src">mumsnet.com/talk · healthunlocked.com/communities</div></div>
  <div class="lens"><div class="nm"><span class="ix">First post</span>Welcome it, per circle</div><p>First-post care is the biggest retention lever — a "congrats on your first post" + "let me know if you have questions" is "the difference between a member who disappears and one who becomes a regular." In an anonymous app the buddy = Jess + a warm auto-welcome.</p><div class="src">fluentcommunity.co/blog/community-onboarding-strategies</div></div>
  <div class="lens"><div class="nm"><span class="ix">Discovery</span>A few for you, whole-life, condition circles last</div><p>"Surface only the most relevant, don't bombard." A short opt-in taste survey is the least-creepy matcher. Crucial guardrail: <b>never infer a condition circle from tracked symptoms</b> (creepy + special-category), and <b>interleave a Shared-loves circle beside any stage/condition one</b> so discovery never reads "here's your illness."</p><div class="src">bettermode.com · fluentcommunity.co</div></div>
  <div class="lens"><div class="nm"><span class="ix">Misinfo</span>The no-upvote quality lever</div><p>Misinformation in condition groups is "a matter of public-health concern"; it's countered by peer correction + surfacing good info. FemWell has <b>no upvotes</b> (k-anon), so it needs a different lever: a calm <b>NHS/charity info anchor</b> per condition circle + easy peer correction + report.</p><div class="src">jmir.org/2025/1/e71140 (scoping review)</div></div>
</div>

<!-- 3 -->
<a id="v1"></a>
<div class="section crimson">
  <div class="eyebrow">3 · The plan</div>
  <h2>Robust v1 — seven pillars</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>#</th><th>Pillar · why</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td><b>Seed every live circle</b> — Jess "first light" post + a circle-specific starter so none is ever empty. <i>(The single highest-impact anti-death move.)</i></td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td><b>Per-circle Jess host + "no post left unanswered"</b> — reuses the backstop just shipped for Talk rooms. A first reply is guaranteed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td><b>Warm first-post welcome</b> — "congrats on your first word" + "you're not too much / it's believed here" (sensitive circles).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td><b>Per-condition NHS/charity info anchor</b> — one calm grounded line atop each condition circle (the misinformation counterweight).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td><b>"A few circles for you" discovery card</b> — whole-life interleaved, condition circles offered gently and last, never inferred from symptoms.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td><b>Author-set content-warning veil</b> on heavy sensitive posts — neutral, "tap to read," never a hard block.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td><b>Circle ritual of the week</b> — one low-barrier rotating prompt per circle (Books: "what wrecked you"; TTC: "one kind thing in the wait"; Peri: "the symptom no one warned you about").</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Plus the shared calm-safety floor already in Community (report/hide in the "…" menu, background moderation, botanical aliases, reaction-only/thread-lock, k-anon, no counts) — and a brand pass so the Circles surfaces match §6.8.2 like the rest.</p>
</div>

<!-- 4 -->
<a id="sensitive"></a>
<div class="section plum">
  <div class="eyebrow">4 · The careful bit</div>
  <h2>Sensitive circles — safe AND warm</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Consent-on-join</b> (built) + add "<b>leaving removes you from the member list</b>" (UK GDPR posture — these are special-category data).</li>
    <li><b>Never infer a condition circle from tracked symptoms</b> — self-declaration or self-open only.</li>
    <li><b>NHS/charity info anchor</b> per condition — the quality lever in a no-upvote app.</li>
    <li><b>Content-warning veil</b> — neutral, preparatory, optional (reduces anxiety-contagion for readers).</li>
    <li><b>Tint the condition circle with life</b> — recipes, dating, work belong in a PCOS circle too, so it never curdles into a symptom feed.</li>
    <li><b>Warm first-timer tone</b> — "believed here," never clinical.</li>
    <li><b>Legal review</b> of the sensitive-consent + single-sex/age wording before public launch (flagged, not legal advice).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- 5 -->
<a id="build"></a>
<div class="section">
  <div class="eyebrow">5 · Build reality</div>
  <h2>What's buildable now vs needs sign-off</h2>
  <p><b>Almost everything is buildable now</b> on the entities we already have — the curated \`CIRCLES\` catalogue, \`CircleMembership\`, circle-scoped \`CommunityPost\`, Jess, and \`suggestedCircles\`. No new DB entity, no external creds. The seeding, host, welcome, info anchor, discovery card and ritual prompts all reuse existing paths.</p>
  <p><span class="pill now">buildable now:</span> seed circles · Jess host + backstop · first-post welcome · info anchor · discovery card · ritual prompts · brand pass.</p>
  <p><span class="pill field">one small schema field (sign-off):</span> the author-set content-warning veil (pillar 6) needs a new <b>\`CommunityPost.content_warning\`</b> field (a lean add, no external creds). Everything else needs nothing.</p>
  <p class="cap"><b>Do NOT add a \`Circle\` entity</b> — the curated-catalogue-in-code decision stays. This pass would be one build on top of the Community safety floor. <b>Nothing here is built yet — awaiting your go on the plan (and the one content-warning field).</b></p>
</div>

<div class="foot">
  FemWell · Community feature #4 · Circles — brainstorm (build paused for reaction) · research: workspace/research_circles_2026-07-09.md<br>
  Reachable from the IDEAS pill → Current
</div>

</div></body></html>
